// SEM-001: a bare trailing dot (`5.`, nothing -- not even a digit -- after
// it) is NOT part of the number at all. FREEZE.md's dotted-float grammar
// requires at least one fractional digit after '.', so `5.` was never a
// valid float form; the lexer only commits to '.' as a literal's decimal
// point when a digit immediately follows it (see the "Dotted form" comment
// in Lexer.cpp's number-scanning branch). `5` is returned as a plain int
// literal and '.' is left for the next token -- which is why this fails
// with a generic, PRE-EXISTING parser diagnostic ("Expected a method name
// after '.'", '.' being read as instance-member access on the int `5`),
// not one of SEM-001's four new lexical-error classes. This test locks in
// that deliberate boundary decision rather than exercising new machinery.

fn main(): int {
    mut x: double = 5.;
    return 0;
}
